    "https://careers.utas.edu.au/en/job/503996/associate-director-alumni-relations": {
      "title": "Jobs and Careers",
      "description": "Current Vacancies - Jobs and Careers | University of Tasmania Close Home Study with us Uni life Our research For community & partners About us Staff portal Student portal MyLO eStudent Search Close How can we help you? Search Back to Working at the University Jobs and Careers Looking to become part of a welcoming and collaborative community making a difference for Tasmania and the World? View our current career opportunities here. Home About us Working at the University Current job opportunities Applicant login Subscribe Search e.g. “Marketing”, “IMAS”, “Research” Filter results Work type Full time 1 Locations Statewide (all locations in Tasmania) 1 Categories Professional Management 1 Associate Director, Alumni Relations Apply now Job no: 503996 Work type: Full time Location: Statewide (all locations in Tasmania) Categories: Management The University of Tasmania acknowledges the palawa/pakana and Gadigal/Wangal people as the traditional custodians of the land, sea and waters of the areas upon which we live and work. We recognise their valuable contributions and deep connection to country and pay respect to Elders past and present . Lead alumni engagement across a global community of 170,000+ graduates Drive advocacy, reputation and affinity through alumni engagement Based in Tasmania with relocation support for the successful candidate About the Opportunity The University is seeking an exceptional leader to provide strategic direction for its alumni engagement portfolio, driving institution-wide initiatives that strengthen reputation, foster advocacy, and support long-term sustainability and growth. As the University's principal alumni engagement leader, you will oversee engagement with a global community of more than 170,000 alumni across 139 countries, fostering lifelong connections, building advocacy, and creating pathways for philanthropic support. You will lead major strategic initiatives, cultivate high-value stakeholder relationships, and work in partnership with senior leaders to deliver meaningful institutional outcomes. As a member of the Advancement Leadership Team, you will provide strategic advice, lead a high-performing team, and deliver transformational programs that enhance the University's impact and standing within the communities it serves. By fostering a globally connected and engaged alumni community, you will help drive the University's strategic priorities, enhance its standing and influence, and secure opportunities that support its future growth and success. Role Specific Skills, Knowledge and Experience Relevant postgraduate qualification and extensive executive-level experience; or an equivalent combination of advanced qualifications, leadership experience and demonstrated organisational impact Extensive executive-level knowledge and experience implementing and leading large-scale alumni engagement, advancement, stakeholder engagement or related strategic portfolios. Excellent relationship management skills and demonstrated ability to influence, negotiate and collaborate with senior stakeholders to achieve strategic objectives. Demonstrated success leading organisational strategy, policy development, major programs and enterprise-wide change initiatives. Outstanding verbal, written and stakeholder communication skills. Demonstrated financial, workforce and resource management capability, including responsibility for significant budgets and strategic investment decisions. Strong analytical and reporting capability, including the use of data, engagement analytics and performance metrics to drive organisational outcomes. Demonstrated ability to exercise independent judgment and provide strategic advice in complex organisational environments. Significant experience working with Advancement CRM databases and engagement reporting systems. Other Requirements Regular intrastate travel to Tasmanian campuses. Regular interstate travel. Occasional international travel. What’s on Offer? In addition, you’ll have access an array of staff benefits and discounts including. 26 Weeks paid parental leave for primary care givers 17% Superannuation contribution Health and fitness membership options Accommodation and Car Hire discounts Health Insurance and Banking Novated Leasing Salary details Appointment to this role will be at HEO 10 and will have a total remuneration package of up to $163,282 comprising base salary within the range of $137,494 to $139,557 plus 17% superannuation. About the University At the University of Tasmania, we have mission to make a difference for Tasmania, and from Tasmania. With our unique location and complex history, we have both an obligation and opportunity to make a global contribution. People are at the heart of that contribution. We focus on building a workplace culture where we come together in a way that empowers us all to connect, belong and grow. The University is one of the largest employers in our State, with campuses in three distinct regions: Hobart, Launceston, and Burnie. We also have a specialised health campus in Sydney. We strive to stimulate economic growth, lift literacy, improve health outcomes for Tasmania and nurture our environment as it nurtures us. We welcome enquiring minds, from near and far, to join us in our pursuit of the extraordinary. How to Apply? If this role is of interest, we look forward to hearing from you. Your application must include: Your up-to-date resume A 2-page cover letter outlining your motivation for the role and includes an overview of how your previous skills & experience aligns to the requirements of the role Please submit your application via our PageUp portal For current UTAS staff, in submitting your application, you acknowledge you have/intend to discuss your interest in this vacancy with your current manager and understand they may need to be contacted confidentially by the hiring manager as part of the assessment of your application. For more information about the role please contact Rebecca Cuthill - Director, Advancement, via email at rebecca.cuthill@utas.edu.au or via phone on +61 0408 012 048. Position Description - Associate Director Alumni Relations - August 2026.pdf Please note applications close on Sunday 13 th of September at 11.55pm. As part of our commitment to a safe and inclusive workplace, employment history and police checks may be conducted as part of the selection process. To be eligible for this position, you are required to hold Australian or New Zealand Citizenship, permanent residency or a valid visa that enables you to fulfil the requirements of this role. Advertised: 18 Aug 2026 Tasmania Standard Time Applications close: 13 Sep 2026 Tasmania Standard Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App Send me jobs like these We will email you new jobs that match this search. Great, we can send you jobs like this, if this is your first time signing up, please check your inbox to confirm your subscription. The email address was invalid, please check for errors. You must agree to the privacy statement Subscribe Recaptcha Privacy agreement Search results Position Location Closes Associate Director, Alumni Relations Statewide (all locations in Tasmania) 13 Sep 2026 Lead the University's global alumni engagement strategy, driving advocacy, reputation, affinity and long-term institutional impact. Expression of interest Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander Expression of Interest Are you an Aboriginal and/or Torres Strait Islander person looking for a dynamic employment opportunity that allows you to contribute and make a difference in your community? Current Opportunities Position Location Closes Associate Director, Alumni Relations Statewide (all locations in Tasmania) 13 Sep 2026 Lead the University's global alumni engagement strategy, driving advocacy, reputation, affinity and long-term institutional impact. Powered by PageUp Authorised by the Director of Marketing. © 2025 University of Tasmania, Australia. ABN 30 764 374 782. CRICOS Provider Code 00586B Copyright statement Disclaimer Privacy Web accessibility"
    },
    "https://careers.utas.edu.au/en/job/504003/senior-research-fellow-policy-impact": {
      "title": "Jobs and Careers",
      "description": "Current Vacancies - Jobs and Careers | University of Tasmania Close Home Study with us Uni life Our research For community & partners About us Staff portal Student portal MyLO eStudent Search Close How can we help you? Search Back to Working at the University Jobs and Careers Looking to become part of a welcoming and collaborative community making a difference for Tasmania and the World? View our current career opportunities here. Home About us Working at the University Current job opportunities Applicant login Subscribe Search e.g. “Marketing”, “IMAS”, “Research” Filter results Work type Full time 1 Locations Hobart 1 Categories Academic Research Focused 1 Senior Research Fellow (Policy Impact) Apply now Job no: 504003 Work type: Full time Location: Hobart Categories: Research Focused The University of Tasmania acknowledges the Palawa/Pakana and Gadigal/Wangal people as the traditional custodians of the land, sea and waters of the areas upon which we live and work. We recognise their valuable contributions and deep connection to country and pay respect to Elders past and present. Lead the development of high-impact applied policy research and delivery of complex projects that inform decision-making and drive meaningful social, economic and environmental outcomes Build and strengthen partnerships with key stakeholders while contributing to the strategic growth, innovation and influence of the Tasmanian Policy Exchange Full time, Fixed-Term (1 year, parental leave) position located in Hobart About the opportunity The Tasmanian Policy Exchange (TPE) connects the University with government, industry and the community to provide independent evidence and policy insights that shape Tasmania’s future. Drawing on expertise from across the University, the Senior Research Fellow (Policy Impact) is an Academic Level C role responsible for leading high-impact applied policy research, developing influential policy advice and publications, managing complex projects, and building strong stakeholder partnerships. Working with a high degree of autonomy, the role contributes to the growth and strategic direction of the TPE and may also support impact leadership, stakeholder engagement, capability development, strategic planning and project oversight to ensure the TPE remains responsive to emerging opportunities while delivering excellence in research, policy innovation and community impact. What you’ll do: Design, lead, and deliver innovative, evidence-based policy research and analysis addressing issues of strategic importance to Tasmania's future. Produce influential policy reports,submissions, briefs and other research outputs that inform policy development, public debate, and reform agendas. Establish and maintain trusted relationships with government, industry, community, and academic partners to support policy innovation, knowledge exchange, and collaborative research. Secure and manage externally funded projects and partnerships that enhance the University's policy impact and research profile. Provide academic and project leadership across multidisciplinary initiatives, fostering excellence, collaboration, and professional growth within project teams and the wider TPE.­ What we’re looking for: A PhD and/or significant professional experience in public policy, political economy, economics, regional development, political science, public administration, or a closely related discipline. Strong record of working with partners to deliver applied policy research and analysis addressing complex public policy challenges. Demonstrated success designing and leading complex research and policy projects from inception through to implementation and impact. Exceptional written and verbal communication skills, including the ability to communicate complex research findings to non-expert audiences. Experience building effective relationships with government, industry, community, and academic stakeholders. Salary details Appointment to this role will be at Academic Level C and will have a total remuneration package of up to $184,076 comprising base salary within the range of $138,536 to $157,330 plus 17% superannuation. How to Apply To apply online, please provide the following supporting documentation: Resume Cover letter outlining your suitability and motivation for the role Your responses to the success criteria from the Position Description below For further information about this position, please contact Robert Hortle, Acting Director, Tasmanian Policy Exchange, robert.hortle@utas.edu.au or 6220 9456 Please visit https://www.utas.edu.au/jobs/applying for our guide to applying and details on the recruitment process. For current UTAS staff, in submitting your application, you acknowledge you have/intend to discuss your interest in this vacancy with your current manager and understand they may need to be contacted confidentially by the hiring manager as part of the assessment of your application. Please refer to the attached Position Description Below for full details. Position Description - Senior Research Fellow (Policy Impact).pdf . Applications close Thursday, 10 September 2026, 11.55pm As part of our commitment to a safe and inclusive workplace, employment history and police checks may be conducted as part of the selection process. To be eligible for this position, you are required to hold Australian or New Zealand Citizenship, permanent residency or a valid visa that enables you to fulfil the requirements of this role. Advertised: 12 Aug 2026 Tasmania Standard Time Applications close: 10 Sep 2026 Tasmania Standard Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App Send me jobs like these We will email you new jobs that match this search. Great, we can send you jobs like this, if this is your first time signing up, please check your inbox to confirm your subscription. The email address was invalid, please check for errors. You must agree to the privacy statement Subscribe Recaptcha Privacy agreement Search results Position Location Closes Senior Research Fellow (Policy Impact) Hobart 10 Sep 2026 Shape Tasmania’s future through impactful research, influential policy analysis and strategic collaboration across the University, government, industry, and community sectors. Expression of interest Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander Expression of Interest Are you an Aboriginal and/or Torres Strait Islander person looking for a dynamic employment opportunity that allows you to contribute and make a difference in your community? Current Opportunities Position Location Closes Senior Research Fellow (Policy Impact) Hobart 10 Sep 2026 Shape Tasmania’s future through impactful research, influential policy analysis and strategic collaboration across the University, government, industry, and community sectors. Powered by PageUp Authorised by the Director of Marketing. © 2025 University of Tasmania, Australia. ABN 30 764 374 782. CRICOS Provider Code 00586B Copyright statement Disclaimer Privacy Web accessibility"
    },
    "https://careers.utas.edu.au/en/job/503941/stock-assessment-scientist": {
      "title": "Jobs and Careers",
      "description": "Current Vacancies - Jobs and Careers | University of Tasmania Close Home Study with us Uni life Our research For community & partners About us Staff portal Student portal MyLO eStudent Search Close How can we help you? Search Back to Working at the University Jobs and Careers Looking to become part of a welcoming and collaborative community making a difference for Tasmania and the World? View our current career opportunities here. Home About us Working at the University Current job opportunities Applicant login Subscribe Search e.g. “Marketing”, “IMAS”, “Research” Filter results Work type Full time 1 Locations Hobart 1 Categories Academic Research Focused 1 Stock Assessment Scientist Apply now Job no: 503941 Work type: Full time Location: Hobart Categories: Research Focused The University of Tasmania acknowledges the palawa/pakana and Gadigal/Wangal people as the traditional custodians of the land, sea and waters of the areas upon which we live and work. We recognise their valuable contributions and deep connection to country and pay respect to Elders past and present. Shape Tasmania's fisheries future through world-leading stock assessment and recovery science Apply advanced quantitative modelling to real-world fisheries management with lasting environmental impact Ongoing, full time role based in Hobart - Relocation considered for the right applicant! About the opportunity Join a team working at the forefront of fisheries science in our Modelling Program. One of your first roles will be to help rebuild one of Tasmania's most significant and highly valued recreational scalefish fisheries. This role offers a unique opportunity to apply cutting-edge quantitative approaches to a high-profile fisheries recovery program that will directly inform management and conservation outcomes. The successful applicant will lead the development and application of advanced stock assessment models, with a particular focus on Management Strategy Evaluation (MSE) and length-based equilibrium modelling for Sand Flathead. These models will underpin stock assessments and evaluate management scenarios designed to achieve rapid recovery to agreed biological reference points. Working closely with collaborators from the Department of Natural Resources and Environment Tasmania, the position will also contribute to the development of a contemporary harvest strategy for the fishery. The role will evolve into developing stock assessment models for a range of different species, supporting the development of harvest strategies for the management of Tasmania’s marine resources. What you’ll do: Lead the development and application of quantitative stock assessment models. Deliver high-quality scientific outputs that directly inform fisheries management. Collaborate effectively with project partners, including the Department of Natural Resources and Environment Tasmania. Provide scientific support within the project, managing modelling workflows, contributing to project planning, communicating complex quantitative analyses to technical and non-technical audiences. Contribute to the University's research excellence and capability.­ What we’re looking for: A PhD or equivalent in fisheries science, quantitative ecology, applied mathematics, statistics, population dynamics or a closely related discipline. Demonstrated expertise in quantitative fisheries stock assessment. Advanced programming and analytical skills, with demonstrated proficiency in R and/or other relevant scientific programming languages. Demonstrated ability to communicate complex quantitative concepts to both technical and non-technical audiences. Experience working collaboratively with government agencies, industry, recreational fishing organisations and other stakeholders. Salary details Appointment to this role will be at Academic Level C and will have a total remuneration package of up to $184,076 comprising base salary within the range of $138,536 to $157,330 plus 17% superannuation. How to Apply To apply online, please provide the following supporting documentation: Resume Your responses to the success criteria from the Position Description below For further information about this position, please contact Katie Cresswell, Stock Assessment Scientist - Benthic Invertebrates, Katie.Cresswell@utas.edu.au Please visit https://www.utas.edu.au/jobs/applying for our guide to applying and details on the recruitment process. For current UTAS staff, in submitting your application, you acknowledge you have/intend to discuss your interest in this vacancy with your current manager and understand they may need to be contacted confidentially by the hiring manager as part of the assessment of your application. Please refer to the attached Position Description Below for full details. Position Description - 503941 - Stock Assessment Scientist.pdf Applications close Tuesday, 8 September 2026, 11.55pm As part of our commitment to a safe and inclusive workplace, employment history and police checks may be conducted as part of the selection process. Advertised: 10 Aug 2026 Tasmania Standard Time Applications close: 08 Sep 2026 Tasmania Standard Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App Send me jobs like these We will email you new jobs that match this search. Great, we can send you jobs like this, if this is your first time signing up, please check your inbox to confirm your subscription. The email address was invalid, please check for errors. You must agree to the privacy statement Subscribe Recaptcha Privacy agreement Search results Position Location Closes Stock Assessment Scientist Hobart 8 Sep 2026 Lead innovative fisheries research at the Institute for Marine and Antarctic Studies as a Stock Assessment Scientist Expression of interest Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander Expression of Interest Are you an Aboriginal and/or Torres Strait Islander person looking for a dynamic employment opportunity that allows you to contribute and make a difference in your community? Current Opportunities Position Location Closes Stock Assessment Scientist Hobart 8 Sep 2026 Lead innovative fisheries research at the Institute for Marine and Antarctic Studies as a Stock Assessment Scientist Powered by PageUp Authorised by the Director of Marketing. © 2025 University of Tasmania, Australia. ABN 30 764 374 782. CRICOS Provider Code 00586B Copyright statement Disclaimer Privacy Web accessibility"
