    "https://careers.utas.edu.au/en/job/504132/principal-hpc-and-storage-architect": {
      "title": "Current Vacancies - Jobs and Careers",
      "description": "Current Vacancies - Jobs and Careers | University of Tasmania Close Home Study with us Uni life Our research For community & partners About us Staff portal Student portal MyLO eStudent Search Close How can we help you? Search Back to Working at the University Jobs and Careers Looking to become part of a welcoming and collaborative community making a difference for Tasmania and the World? View our current career opportunities here. Home About us Working at the University Current job opportunities Applicant login Subscribe Search e.g. “Marketing”, “IMAS”, “Research” Filter results Work type Full time 1 Locations Hobart 1 Categories Professional Information Technology 1 Principal HPC and Storage Architect Apply now Job no: 504132 Work type: Full time Location: Hobart Categories: Information Technology The University of Tasmania acknowledges the Palawa/Pakana and Gadigal/Wangal people as the traditional custodians of the land, sea and waters of the areas upon which we live and work. We recognise their valuable contributions and deep connection to country and pay respect to Elders past and present. Lead the design, delivery and strategic direction of the University of Tasmania's next-generation High-Performance Computing (HPC) and research data storage environment Work with leading researchers and national research partners to support globally connected research Full time, ongoing role based in Hobart About the opportunity The Principal HPC and Storage Architect is the University's senior technical authority for research computing and data storage architecture within Digital Research, IT Services. The role will lead the end-to-end design, procurement, deployment and commissioning of the University's new high-performance computing platform, while setting the target architecture and lifecycle roadmap for the integrated storage ecosystem, including Ceph, HPSS, tape and S3 compatible object storage. Working with researchers, technical specialists, Cyber Security, enterprise IT, vendors and national partners, the position will translate complex and emerging research requirements into secure, resilient, scalable and cost-effective infrastructure. Operating under broad direction and with considerable autonomy, the role will establish architecture standards, provide design assurance, guide major investment decisions and lead multidisciplinary delivery teams. The role manages and develops specialist staff and enables data-intensive, reproducible and globally connected research for Tasmania and from Tasmania to the world. What you’ll do: Develop and maintain the roadmap for Ceph, HPSS, tape, object storage and data movement services, including storage tiers and data lifecycle pathways from active use through to archive and publication. Establish technical standards, principles, performance models and documentation requirements, and ensure solutions meet security, interoperability, sustainability and supportability requirements. Coordinate compute, storage, networking, cloud, identity, backup, archive, cybersecurity and data centre services, including resilience, recovery and testing activities. Develop roadmaps, business cases, cost models, refresh strategies and risk advice, support procurement and vendor management, and provide recommendations to senior stakeholders. Lead and develop HPC and Storage specialists, engage with researchers to translate requirements into scalable services, and represent the University in national research computing and data infrastructure forums. ­ What we’re looking for: Extensive experience as a systems architect, principal engineer, infrastructure lead or senior technical manager responsible for the architecture, governance and delivery of complex high-performance computing and large-scale research data infrastructure in a university, research or comparable enterprise environment. Deep expertise in Linux and HPC architecture, including heterogeneous CPU/GPU compute, workload scheduling, high-speed interconnects, parallel or distributed file systems, cluster management, artificial intelligence tools and techniques, performance engineering, benchmarking and capacity planning. Advanced experience designing and operating distributed, object and archival storage services. Strong experience with Ceph is expected; experience with HPSS, enterprise tape libraries, OpenStack Swift or S3-compatible object storage, and high-performance data-movement tooling is highly desirable. Proven success leading the end-to-end technical delivery of major infrastructure investments, including requirements discovery, options analysis, procurement specifications and evaluation, vendor design review, implementation, migration, commissioning, acceptance testing and transition to service. Demonstrated capacity to conceptualise, develop and review strategic and operational plans, target-state architectures, reference patterns, technology standards, lifecycle roadmaps, whole-of-life cost models, business cases, technical risk assessments and design assurance documentation. Salary details Appointment to this role will be at HEO 9 and will have a total remuneration package of up to $160,613 comprising base salary within the range of $130,242 to $137,276 plus 17% superannuation. How to Apply To apply online, please provide your CV and 1-2-page cover letter outlining your interest in the role, skills, capabilities and experience.  You do not need to separately address the success profile. For further information about this position, please contact Mona Taylor, Manager Digital Research Infrastructure and Cyber Security, Mona.Taylor@utas.edu.au Please visit https://www.utas.edu.au/jobs/applying for our guide to applying and details on the recruitment process. For current UTAS staff, in submitting your application, you acknowledge you have/intend to discuss your interest in this vacancy with your current manager and understand they may need to be contacted confidentially by the hiring manager as part of the assessment of your application. Please refer to the attached Position Description Below for full details. Position Description - 504132 - Principal HPC and Storage Architect.pdf Applications close Monday, 28 September 2026, 11.55pm As part of our commitment to a safe and inclusive workplace, employment history and police checks may be conducted as part of the selection process. To be eligible for this position, you are required to hold Australian or New Zealand Citizenship, permanent residency or a valid visa that enables you to fulfil the requirements of this role. Advertised: 04 Sep 2026 Tasmania Standard Time Applications close: 28 Sep 2026 Tasmania Standard Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App Send me jobs like these We will email you new jobs that match this search. Great, we can send you jobs like this, if this is your first time signing up, please check your inbox to confirm your subscription. The email address was invalid, please check for errors. You must agree to the privacy statement Subscribe Recaptcha Privacy agreement Search results Position Location Closes Principal HPC and Storage Architect Hobart 28 Sep 2026 Build the engine behind discovery - Lead the future of research computing at the University of Tasmania Expression of interest Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander Expression of Interest Are you an Aboriginal and/or Torres Strait Islander person looking for a dynamic employment opportunity that allows you to contribute and make a difference in your community? Current Opportunities Position Location Closes Principal HPC and Storage Architect Hobart 28 Sep 2026 Build the engine behind discovery - Lead the future of research computing at the University of Tasmania Powered by PageUp Authorised by the Director of Marketing. © 2025 University of Tasmania, Australia. ABN 30 764 374 782. CRICOS Provider Code 00586B Copyright statement Disclaimer Privacy Web accessibility"
    },
    "https://careers.utas.edu.au/en/job/503786/head-school-of-psychological-sciences": {
      "title": "Current Vacancies - Jobs and Careers",
      "description": "Current Vacancies - Jobs and Careers | University of Tasmania Close Home Study with us Uni life Our research For community & partners About us Staff portal Student portal MyLO eStudent Search Close How can we help you? Search Back to Working at the University Jobs and Careers Looking to become part of a welcoming and collaborative community making a difference for Tasmania and the World? View our current career opportunities here. Home About us Working at the University Current job opportunities Applicant login Subscribe Search e.g. “Marketing”, “IMAS”, “Research” Filter results Work type Full time 1 Locations Hobart 1 Launceston 1 Statewide (all locations in Tasmania) 1 Categories Academic Balanced Research/Teaching 1 Head, School of Psychological Sciences Apply now Job no: 503786 Work type: Full time Location: Hobart, Launceston, Statewide (all locations in Tasmania) Categories: Balanced Research/Teaching The University of Tasmania acknowledges the Palawa/Pakana and Gadigal/Wangal people as the traditional custodians of the land, sea and waters of the areas upon which we live and work. We recognise their valuable contributions and deep connection to country and pay respect to Elders past and present. Build partnerships across health, government, industry and community. Shape future workforce capability and health sector responsiveness. Foster a high-performing, inclusive and collaborative culture. About the Opportunity We are looking to appoint an exceptional academic leader as Head of the School of Psychological Sciences to provide strategic, academic and operational leadership across teaching and learning, research, and community and industry engagement. In this role, you will shape the School's future direction by leading the development of sustainable and innovative academic programs, strengthening and aligning research performance and impact, and building meaningful partnerships across government, health services, industry and community sectors. As a key member of the UTAS Health leadership team, you will support the stewardship of the Health portfolio and broader institutional priorities, and help advance the University's Health Transition. Working collaboratively across the University, you will champion interdisciplinary approaches, support staff and student success, and position the School to respond to emerging workforce, professional practice and community needs. This is a unique opportunity to lead a School that plays a vital role in advancing health and wellbeing outcomes for, and from, Tasmania. Through strategic leadership and collaboration, you will help shape the future of Psychological Sciences, strengthen the University's impact, and make a lasting contribution to the communities it serves. About the School of Psychological Sciences As part of UTAS Health, the School of Psychological Sciences is a vibrant and high-performing academic community with a strong reputation for excellence in teaching, research and community engagement. The School delivers nationally accredited undergraduate, Honours and postgraduate programs in Psychology, alongside Counselling programs offered across Tasmania and nationally, providing accessible and innovative pathways for students seeking to make a meaningful difference in people's lives. Through a commitment to outstanding student experiences, industry engagement and evidence-based practice, the School prepares highly skilled graduates to understand, assess and influence human behaviour across a broad range of professional, clinical and community settings. Close connections with government, health and community sectors, together with the University Psychology Clinics in Hobart and Launceston, create rich opportunities for applied learning, research translation and community impact. About the University The University of Tasmania is a mission-driven institution dedicated to making a difference for Lutruwita/Tasmania and a distinctive contribution from Tasmania to the world. As the sole university on the island, the University is deeply embedded in the social, economic, and environmental fabric of Tasmania, working in close partnership with communities, industry, and government to address key challenges in education, health, productivity, and climate action. The University takes pride in its place-based identity, leveraging Tasmania’s unique geography, culture, and resources to offer world-leading research and education. What We are Looking For We are seeking an accomplished and collaborative leader with academic qualifications and experience commensurate with a senior academic appointment in a relevant discipline. You will bring a strong track record of leadership within a university, health education or related setting, with the ability to build high-performing teams, foster an inclusive culture, and contribute to institution-wide outcomes. As the successful candidate will possess a strong understanding of the health workforce, accreditation and service delivery trends shaping higher education. You will demonstrate excellence in academic leadership, including curriculum development, teaching quality, student success, research performance, and partnership development. A strategic and collegiate leader, you will be comfortable navigating complexity, contributing as part of a high-performing leadership team, building relationships across industry, government and community sectors, and overseeing financial, governance and risk responsibilities to ensure a sustainable and future-focused School. What’s on Offer? As we look toward 2050, we remain committed to fostering excellence, collaboration, and transformative education - preparing graduates to navigate the challenges of a rapidly changing world, and shape its future. While remaining true to our Tasmanian roots, this career defining role will see you significantly contribute to our dedication to making a difference for, and from, Tasmania. In return for your experience, you’ll have access to an array of staff benefits and discounts including: 26 Weeks paid parental leave for primary care givers 17% Superannuation contribution Health and fitness membership options Accommodation and Car Hire discounts Health Insurance and Banking Novated Leasing Generous relocation support to Tasmania How to Apply? If this role is of interest, we look forward to hearing from you. Please provide your up-to-date resume and: A 1-2 page cover letter outlining your motivation for the role at the University of Tasmania Address the selection criteria by outlining how your skills and experience align with the role specific skills, knowledge and experience in the Position Description Applications close 11.55pm Sunday the 27th of September. For further information about this position, please contact the Talent Acquisition team via email: TalentTeam@utas.edu.au Position Description - Head of Psychological Sciences.pdf To be eligible for this position, you are required to have Australian citizenship, permanent residence or a current valid visa that allows you to fulfill the requirements of this role. As part of our commitment to a safe and inclusive workplace, employment history and police background checks may be conducted as part of the selection process. Advertised: 03 Sep 2026 Tasmania Standard Time Applications close: 27 Sep 2026 Tasmania Standard Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App Send me jobs like these We will email you new jobs that match this search. Great, we can send you jobs like this, if this is your first time signing up, please check your inbox to confirm your subscription. The email address was invalid, please check for errors. You must agree to the privacy statement Subscribe Recaptcha Privacy agreement Search results Position Location Closes Head, School of Psychological Sciences Hobart, Launceston, Statewide (all locations in Tasmania) 27 Sep 2026 Lead the strategic direction of Psychological Sciences, advancing education, research, partnerships and impact. Expression of interest Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander Expression of Interest Are you an Aboriginal and/or Torres Strait Islander person looking for a dynamic employment opportunity that allows you to contribute and make a difference in your community? Current Opportunities Position Location Closes Head, School of Psychological Sciences Hobart, Launceston, Statewide (all locations in Tasmania) 27 Sep 2026 Lead the strategic direction of Psychological Sciences, advancing education, research, partnerships and impact. Powered by PageUp Authorised by the Director of Marketing. © 2025 University of Tasmania, Australia. ABN 30 764 374 782. CRICOS Provider Code 00586B Copyright statement Disclaimer Privacy Web accessibility"
    },
    "https://careers.utas.edu.au/en/job/504144/research-fellow-digital-forests": {
      "title": "Research Fellow Digital Forests",
      "description": "Current Vacancies - Jobs and Careers | University of Tasmania Close Home Study with us Uni life Our research For community & partners About us Staff portal Student portal MyLO eStudent Search Close How can we help you? Search Back to Working at the University Jobs and Careers Looking to become part of a welcoming and collaborative community making a difference for Tasmania and the World? View our current career opportunities here. Home About us Working at the University Current job opportunities Applicant login Subscribe Search e.g. “Marketing”, “IMAS”, “Research” Filter results Work type Full time 1 Locations Hobart 1 Statewide (all locations in Tasmania) 1 Categories Academic Research Intensive 1 Research Fellow Digital Forests Apply now Job no: 504144 Work type: Full time Location: Statewide (all locations in Tasmania), Hobart Categories: Research Intensive The University of Tasmania acknowledges the Palawa/Pakana and Gadigal/Wangal people as the traditional custodians of the land, sea and waters of the areas upon which we live and work. We recognise their valuable contributions and deep connection to country and pay respect to Elders past and present. · Drive innovation in digital forestry and geospatial analytics · Work with industry to translate research into operational outcomes · Relocation support may be available for suitable candidates moving to Tasmania About the Opportunity We are seeking to appoint a Senior Research Fellow or Principal Research Fellow, Digital Forests to join the Australian Forest and Wood Innovations (AFWI) Centre for Sustainable Resource and Product Solutions. This fixed-term appointment offers a unique opportunity to shape the future of digital forest monitoring and inventory across Australia. Over the next four years, you will help bridge the gap between emerging sensing technologies and their practical application in forest management, planning and reporting. Working at the intersection of research, technology and industry, you will lead the development of innovative approaches using multi-sensor lidar, imaging analytics and geospatial data to improve forest inventory, monitoring and decision-making. We welcome applications from candidates with experience across academia, industry, consulting or government. Whether your expertise has been developed through applied research, operational forestry, environmental consulting or public sector leadership, this role offers the opportunity to deliver nationally significant impact through collaboration, innovation and real-world application. About the University The University of Tasmania is a mission-driven institution dedicated to making a difference for Lutruwita/Tasmania and a distinctive contribution from Tasmania to the world. As the sole university on the island, the University is deeply embedded in the social, economic, and environmental fabric of Tasmania, working in close partnership with communities, industry, and government to address key challenges in education, health, productivity, and climate action. The University takes pride in its place-based identity, leveraging Tasmania’s unique geography, culture, and resources to offer world-leading research and education. What We are Looking For We welcome applications from individuals with deep expertise in digital forestry, remote sensing, geospatial analytics, environmental data science or related disciplines. While a PhD is valued, we recognise that expertise can be developed through a variety of career pathways. We strongly encourage applications from candidates whose experience has been built in industry, consulting, government agencies or applied research environments. The successful candidate will bring a strong track record of translating technical innovation into practical outcomes and working collaboratively with stakeholders to address real-world challenges in forest inventory, monitoring and management. You will have demonstrated leadership experience, including leading projects and teams, building productive partnerships, mentoring others, securing funding or commercial work, and delivering outcomes across complex organisational environments. We are seeking a leader who can bridge the gap between emerging technologies and industry adoption, engaging confidently with both technical specialists and operational decision-makers. The role may be appointed at either Senior Research Fellow or Principal Research Fellow level. Please refer to the relevant Position Description for the full role requirements, including the qualifications, skills, experience and leadership capabilities sought at each level. What’s on Offer? As we look toward 2050, we remain committed to fostering excellence, collaboration, and transformative education - preparing graduates to navigate the challenges of a rapidly changing world, and shape its future. While remaining true to our Tasmanian roots, this career defining role will see you significantly contribute to our dedication to making a difference for, and from, Tasmania. In return for your experience, you’ll have access to an array of staff benefits and discounts including: · 26 Weeks paid parental leave for primary care givers · 17% Superannuation contribution · Health and fitness membership options · Accommodation and Car Hire discounts · Health Insurance and Banking · Novated Leasing · Relocation Support Salary Details Appointment to this role will be at Academic Level C or D and will have a total remuneration package of up to $210,621 comprising base salary within the range of $138,536 to $180,018 plus 17% superannuation. Relocation support may be available to the successful candidate, helping make a move to Tasmania as seamless as possible. How to Apply? If this role is of interest, we look forward to hearing from you. Please provide your up-to-date resume and: · A cover letter (maximum two pages) outlining your interest in the role and how your skills, knowledge and experience align with the role-specific requirements detailed in the Position Description. · Applications close 11.55pm Sunday the 18 of October. · For general enquiries about the recruitment process, the University, relocation support, or employment opportunities at the University of Tasmania, please contact the Talent Acquisition Team at TalentTeam@utas.edu.au . · For role-specific enquiries, please contact Arko Lucieer, Head of School, Geography, Planning and Spatial Sciences, via email at arko.lucieer@utas.edu.au . Position Description-Level-C AFWI DigitalForests .pdf Position Description-Level D AFWI DigitalForests.pdf As part of our commitment to a safe and inclusive workplace, employment history and police background checks may be conducted as part of the selection process. Advertised: 18 Sep 2026 Tasmania Standard Time Applications close: 18 Oct 2026 Tasmania Daylight Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App Send me jobs like these We will email you new jobs that match this search. Great, we can send you jobs like this, if this is your first time signing up, please check your inbox to confirm your subscription. The email address was invalid, please check for errors. You must agree to the privacy statement Subscribe Recaptcha Privacy agreement Search results Position Location Closes Research Fellow Digital Forests Statewide (all locations in Tasmania), Hobart 18 Oct 2026 Lead nationally significant digital forestry research, industry partnerships and innovation that improves forest monitoring and management. Expression of interest Loading... Current Opportunities Position Location Closes Research Fellow Digital Forests Statewide (all locations in Tasmania), Hobart 18 Oct 2026 Lead nationally significant digital forestry research, industry partnerships and innovation that improves forest monitoring and management. Powered by PageUp Authorised by the Director of Marketing. © 2025 University of Tasmania, Australia. ABN 30 764 374 782. CRICOS Provider Code 00586B Copyright statement Disclaimer Privacy Web accessibility"
    },
    "https://careers.utas.edu.au/en/job/504247/associate-professorprofessor-in-occupational-therapy-academic-lead": {
      "title": "Associate Professor/Professor in Occupational Therapy",
      "description": "Current Vacancies - Jobs and Careers | University of Tasmania Close Home Study with us Uni life Our research For community & partners About us Staff portal Student portal MyLO eStudent Search Close How can we help you? Search Back to Working at the University Jobs and Careers Looking to become part of a welcoming and collaborative community making a difference for Tasmania and the World? View our current career opportunities here. Home About us Working at the University Current job opportunities Applicant login Subscribe Search e.g. “Marketing”, “IMAS”, “Research” Filter results Work type Full time 1 Locations Launceston 1 Statewide (all locations in Tasmania) 1 Categories Academic Balanced Research/Teaching 1 Associate Professor/Professor in Occupational Therapy - Academic Lead Apply now Job no: 504247 Work type: Full time Location: Launceston, Statewide (all locations in Tasmania) Categories: Balanced Research/Teaching The University of Tasmania acknowledges the Palawa/Pakana and Gadigal/Wangal people as the traditional custodians of the land, sea and waters of the areas upon which we live and work. We recognise their valuable contributions and deep connection to country and pay respect to Elders past and present. · Permanent academic leadership role located in Launceston. · Drive excellence in Occupational Therapy education, research and workforce development. · Work within The Shed, UTAS Health's state-of-the-art teaching, simulation and research environment. About the Opportunity We are seeking to appoint an outstanding Professor or Associate Professor as Academic Lead in Occupational Therapy to play a pivotal role in continuing to shape the future of occupational therapy education, research and workforce development across Tasmania. This is a unique opportunity for an accomplished academic leader to make a significant strategic contribution through a holistic portfolio spanning program leadership, curriculum innovation, stakeholder engagement, workforce development and teaching and research excellence. Leading the Master of Occupational Therapy program, the successful candidate will work collaboratively with government, health services, industry partners and local communities to strengthen allied health capability across the state. As Academic Lead, they will drive program quality, accreditation, growth and student success while fostering a high-performing and collaborative academic culture. This appointment presents an opportunity to play a leading role in advancing allied health education and workforce capability across Tasmania. Working in partnership with government, health services, industry and community stakeholders, the successful candidate will help shape innovative educational pathways, strengthen interdisciplinary collaboration and support the development of a sustainable allied health workforce. Their contribution will have a lasting impact on health outcomes and service delivery for Tasmanian communities. About the University The University of Tasmania is a mission-driven institution dedicated to making a difference for Lutruwita/Tasmania and a distinctive contribution from Tasmania to the world. As the sole university on the island, the University is deeply embedded in the social, economic, and environmental fabric of Tasmania, working in close partnership with communities, industry, and government to address key challenges in education, health, productivity, and climate action. The University takes pride in its place-based identity, leveraging Tasmania’s unique geography, culture, and resources to offer world-leading research and education. What We are Looking For? We are seeking a highly skilled and accomplished academic leader with demonstrated expertise in occupational therapy, education and research. The successful candidate will bring a strong track record of leadership, strategic relationship building and innovation, with the ability to lead programs, inspire teams and drive meaningful outcomes. They will be passionate about advancing the profession, shaping future workforce capability and creating lasting impact through education, research and community engagement. What’s on Offer? As we look toward 2050, we remain committed to fostering excellence, collaboration, and transformative education - preparing graduates to navigate the challenges of a rapidly changing world, and shape its future. While remaining true to our Tasmanian roots, this career defining role will see you significantly contribute to our dedication to making a difference for, and from, Tasmania. In return for your experience, you’ll have access to an array of staff benefits and discounts including: · 26 Weeks paid parental leave for primary care givers · 17% Superannuation contribution · Health and fitness membership options · Accommodation and Car Hire discounts · Health Insurance and Banking · Novated Leasing · Generous relocation support to Tasmania Salary Details Appointment to this role will be at Academic Level D or E and will have a total remuneration package of up to $244,987 comprising base salary within the range of $166,014 to $209,391 plus 17% superannuation. Relocation support may be available to the successful candidate, helping make a move to Tasmania as seamless as possible. How to Apply? If this role is of interest, we look forward to hearing from you. Please provide your up-to-date resume and: · A 1-page cover letter outlining your motivation for the role at the University of Tasmania · A separate addressal of the selection criteria, outlining how your skills and experience align with the role specific skills, knowledge and experience listed below, in relation to which position you are applying for: Role Specific Skills, Knowledge and Experience Academic Level D · Demonstrated leadership and recognised standing in occupational therapy practice, education and/or research, with evidence of sustained professional engagement and contribution to the advancement of the discipline. · Demonstrated leadership in curriculum strategy, course development and educational innovation, including the implementation of evidence-informed pedagogies, simulation-based education and emerging digital technologies across programs. · Extensive experience leading high-quality coursework learning and teaching initiatives, including curriculum transformation, quality enhancement, accreditation activities and the achievement of outstanding student outcomes. · Nationally and internationally recognised expertise in occupational therapy across the lifespan, including occupational performance, participation, health and wellbeing, with an established profile in one or more specialist areas of practice, education and/or research. · An internationally recognised research profile in occupational therapy or a related field, demonstrated through leadership of significant research programs, success in securing competitive funding, influential publications, successful HDR supervision and completion, and the development of research collaborations and teams. · Demonstrated academic leadership, including mentoring academic staff, leading multidisciplinary teams and contributing to organisational development and academic culture. · Demonstrated success in establishing and sustaining productive national and international collaborations with academic, healthcare, professional and community partners that generate substantial teaching, research and engagement outcomes. Academic Level E · Demonstrated distinction and leadership in occupational therapy practice, education and research, with a sustained record of advancing the profession and influencing policy, practice, education and/or research nationally and internationally. · An outstanding record of leadership in higher education curriculum innovation, educational strategy and learning and teaching excellence, with evidence of significant influence on institutional, national and/or international educational practice, accreditation standards or policy. · A distinguished record of leadership in learning and teaching, including major contributions to curriculum transformation, educational quality enhancement, workforce development and student success at institutional, national and/or international levels. · Internationally distinguished expertise in in occupational therapy across the lifespan, including occupational performance, participation, health and wellbeing, evidenced through sustained and influential contributions to the advancement of the discipline, profession and broader community. · An internationally distinguished record of research achievement and impact in occupational therapy or a related field, demonstrated by leadership of major research programs, centres or networks; substantial success in securing competitive funding; highly influential scholarly outputs; successful development of research capability; and significant impact on policy, professional practice and societal outcomes. · Exceptional leadership, mentoring and strategic relationship-building capabilities, with a demonstrated record of developing future academic leaders, building organisational capability and shaping the strategic direction of academic programs and research agendas. · An outstanding record of establishing and leading strategic national and international partnerships with universities, healthcare services, professional bodies, industry and community organisations that enhance institutional reputation and generate significant educational, research and community impact. Qualifications and Licences · Completion of a PhD Degree in a relevant health discipline. · Current registration as an Occupational Therapist with the Occupational Therapy Board, under the auspices of the Australian Health Practitioner Regulatory Authority (AHPRA). Applicants are strongly encouraged to contact Angela Berndt, Academic Lead, Occupational Therapy, prior to submitting an application to learn more about the role. Angela can be contacted at angela.berndt@utas.edu.au . Position Description - Associate Professor and Academic Lead of Occupational Therapy .pdf Position Description - Professor and Academic Lead of Occupational Therapy .pdf Applications close 11.55pm Sunday the 11th of October. To be eligible for this position, you are required to have Australian citizenship, permanent residence or a current valid visa that allows you to fulfill the requirements of this role. As part of our commitment to a safe and inclusive workplace, employment history and police background checks may be conducted as part of the selection process. Advertised: 18 Sep 2026 Tasmania Standard Time Applications close: 11 Oct 2026 Tasmania Daylight Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App Send me jobs like these We will email you new jobs that match this search. Great, we can send you jobs like this, if this is your first time signing up, please check your inbox to confirm your subscription. The email address was invalid, please check for errors. You must agree to the privacy statement Subscribe Recaptcha Privacy agreement Search results Position Location Closes Associate Professor/Professor in Occupational Therapy - Academic Lead Launceston, Statewide (all locations in Tasmania) 11 Oct 2026 Lead Occupational Therapy education, research and workforce development while shaping the future of allied health in Tasmania. Expression of interest Loading... Current Opportunities Position Location Closes Associate Professor/Professor in Occupational Therapy - Academic Lead Launceston, Statewide (all locations in Tasmania) 11 Oct 2026 Lead Occupational Therapy education, research and workforce development while shaping the future of allied health in Tasmania. Powered by PageUp Authorised by the Director of Marketing. © 2025 University of Tasmania, Australia. ABN 30 764 374 782. CRICOS Provider Code 00586B Copyright statement Disclaimer Privacy Web accessibility"
