    "https://careers.utas.edu.au/en/job/504207/manager-operational-resilience": {
      "title": "Current Vacancies - Jobs and Careers",
      "description": "Current Vacancies - Jobs and Careers | University of Tasmania Close Home Study with us Uni life Our research For community & partners About us Staff portal Student portal MyLO eStudent Search Close How can we help you? Search Back to Working at the University Jobs and Careers Looking to become part of a welcoming and collaborative community making a difference for Tasmania and the World? View our current career opportunities here. Home About us Working at the University Current job opportunities Applicant login Subscribe Search e.g. “Marketing”, “IMAS”, “Research” Filter results Work type Full time 1 Locations Burnie 1 Hobart 1 Launceston 1 Statewide (all locations in Tasmania) 1 Categories Professional Community & Engagement 1 Infrastructure & Property 1 Strategy 1 Manager Operational Resilience Apply now Job no: 504207 Work type: Full time Location: Hobart, Launceston, Burnie, Statewide (all locations in Tasmania) Categories: Infrastructure & Property, Strategy, Community & Engagement Coordinate the University's Crisis Management and Recovery Team Leads initiatives that strengthen organisational resilience and preparedness. Full Time, Ongoing role based in Hobart, Launceston, or Burnie The University of Tasmania acknowledges the Palawa/Pakana and Gadigal/Wangal people as the traditional custodians of the land, sea and waters of the areas upon which we live and work. We recognise their valuable contributions and deep connection to country and pay respect to Elders past and present. About the opportunity The Manager, Operational Resilience leads the University’s operational resilience, safety and security functions, strengthening the institution’s ability to prepare for, respond to and recover from disruptions. The role oversees business continuity, crisis and emergency management, safety and security operations, and associated risk and compliance activities. Working across the University and with external stakeholders, the position drives organisational preparedness, manages complex incidents, and provides strategic advice to ensure resilience capability remains effective, contemporary and aligned with the University’s priorities. Our ideal candidate will operate at an executive and operational level with experience in emergency management, business continuity, security, risk or organisational resilience within a large and complex organisation. You will be a trusted and influential professional who remains calm under pressure, exercises sound judgement, manages sensitive matters with discretion, and leads initiatives that strengthen organisational resilience and preparedness. What you’ll do: Develop, Maintain and improve the University’s resilience, business continuity, recovery and preparedness framework. Lead the University's response capability before, during and after significant incidents and disruptions. Identify emerging threats and risks and providing advice to support informed decision-making. Oversee safety and security functions and manage complex incidents involving the University community. Ensure frameworks are compliant, understood and embedded across the University. ­ What we’re looking for: Degree qualification in emergency management, risk management, security management, organisational resilience, or a related field; or equivalent experience, accreditation, and/or demonstrated professional expertise. Demonstrated success in leading teams and managing external providers within a large, complex organisation, with a strong understanding of contemporary risk and resilience practices and relevant legislation, regulations, codes, and standards. A confident and effective communicator with strong written and verbal communication skills, including the ability to prepare and present professional reports. Demonstrated high-level analytical and problem-solving skills, with the ability to assess complex information, evaluate evidence, and provide independent, well-informed advice. Highly organised and self-motivated, with a proven ability to work both independently and collaboratively within a small professional team, while managing multiple competing priorities and deadlines in a dynamic environment. Salary details Appointment to this role will be at HEO 10 and will have a total remuneration package of up to $163,282 comprising base salary within the range of $137,494 to $139,557 plus 17% superannuation. How to Apply To apply online, please provide your CV and 1-2-page cover letter outlining your interest in the role, skills, capabilities and experience.  You do not need to separately address the success profile. For further information about this position, please contact Michael Bird, Director Facilities Management, on M.Bird@utas.edu.au or 03 63243767. Please visit https://www.utas.edu.au/jobs/applying for our guide to applying and details on the recruitment process. For current UTAS staff, in submitting your application, you acknowledge you have/intend to discuss your interest in this vacancy with your current manager and understand they may need to be contacted confidentially by the hiring manager as part of the assessment of your application. Please refer to the attached Position Description Below for full details. Position Description - 504207 - Manager Operational Resilience.pdf Applications close Monday 12 October 2026, 11.55pm As part of our commitment to a safe and inclusive workplace, employment history and police checks may be conducted as part of the selection process. To be eligible for this position, you are required to hold Australian or New Zealand Citizenship, permanent residency or a valid visa that enables you to fulfil the requirements of this role. Advertised: 23 Sep 2026 Tasmania Standard Time Applications close: 12 Oct 2026 Tasmania Daylight Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App Send me jobs like these We will email you new jobs that match this search. Great, we can send you jobs like this, if this is your first time signing up, please check your inbox to confirm your subscription. The email address was invalid, please check for errors. You must agree to the privacy statement Subscribe Recaptcha Privacy agreement Search results Position Location Closes Manager Operational Resilience Hobart, Launceston, Burnie, Statewide (all locations in Tasmania) 12 Oct 2026 Lead the University’s resilience, safety and security functions, protecting our people and strengthening preparedness for disruption Expression of interest Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander Expression of Interest Are you an Aboriginal and/or Torres Strait Islander person looking for a dynamic employment opportunity that allows you to contribute and make a difference in your community? Current Opportunities Position Location Closes Manager Operational Resilience Hobart, Launceston, Burnie, Statewide (all locations in Tasmania) 12 Oct 2026 Lead the University’s resilience, safety and security functions, protecting our people and strengthening preparedness for disruption Powered by PageUp Authorised by the Director of Marketing. © 2025 University of Tasmania, Australia. ABN 30 764 374 782. CRICOS Provider Code 00586B Copyright statement Disclaimer Privacy Web accessibility"
    },
    "https://careers.utas.edu.au/en/job/504312/associate-lecturer-in-seafaring-marine-engineering": {
      "title": "Associate Lecturer in Seafaring (Marine Engineering)",
      "description": "Current Vacancies - Jobs and Careers | University of Tasmania Close Home Study with us Uni life Our research For community & partners About us Staff portal Student portal MyLO eStudent Search Close How can we help you? Search Back to Working at the University Jobs and Careers Looking to become part of a welcoming and collaborative community making a difference for Tasmania and the World? View our current career opportunities here. Home About us Working at the University Current job opportunities Applicant login Subscribe Search e.g. “Marketing”, “IMAS”, “Research” Filter results Work type Full time 1 Locations Launceston 1 Categories Academic Teaching Focused 1 Associate Lecturer in Seafaring (Marine Engineering) Apply now Job no: 504312 Work type: Full time Location: Launceston Categories: Teaching Focused The University of Tasmania acknowledges the Palawa/Pakana and Gadigal/Wangal people as the traditional custodians of the land, sea and waters of the areas upon which we live and work. We recognise their valuable contributions and deep connection to country and pay respect to Elders past and present. Bring your marine engineering experience ashore and help prepare the next generation of seafarers Join the internationally recognised Australian Maritime College and help shape the skills and careers of future seafarers Full time, ongoing role based at AMC in Launceston About the opportunity We are looking for a maritime professional with relevant seafaring experience and the requisite Certificate of Competency to join our Ocean Seafaring team at the Australian Maritime College (AMC) as an Associate Lecturer in Seafaring (Marine Engineering). This is an opportunity to share your industry knowledge and experience while inspiring and influencing current and future seafarers through high-quality, industry-relevant training. This role involves teaching and academic administration duties across deck, engineering, and safety subjects. The nature of the teaching will include preparing and delivering theory lessons, tutorials, practical training sessions (with both simulation and live vessels), and the associated assessment development, marking and moderation. The role is based in Launceston and will also include training delivery at our Beauty Point and Bell Bay campuses. Field trips to industry settings, interstate travel and occasional international travel may be required. What you’ll do: Make an effective and sustained contribution to the University in achieving its strategic objectives and fulfilling its operational responsibilities. Develop education, training and assessment resources suitable for both face-to-face and online delivery and undertake moderation of these resources. Safely co-ordinate and conduct lectures, tutorials, practical classes, workshops, field excursions and assessments in both in-person and online environments, prioritising positive student experience. Contribute to the research/scholarship outputs of the AMC’s Centre for Seafaring and Maritime Operations. Contribute to the development and maintenance of productive and effective links inside the University, and locally and nationally with the discipline, the profession, industry and the wider community ­ What we’re looking for: A demonstrated ability to prepare and conduct training using a range of innovative and flexible delivery and assessment methods, and to prepare and conduct curriculum, teaching and training, practical classes, field excursions. and to assess student achievement. A demonstrated ability to work successfully in a team environment, using excellent interpersonal skills. Demonstrated high level written and oral communication skills. A strong record of relevant sea service within the international maritime sector, including experience that supports teaching across Marine Engineering Knowledge, Ship Design, and Shipboard operations. Qualifications and Licence: An AMSA-recognised Marine Engineer Class 1 (Motor, or combined Motor and Steam) certificate of competency (CoC). A Certificate IV in Training and Assessment (or the ability and willingness to obtain within six months of commencement), or other relevant education and training qualification/s, and experience in education, training and assessment. A current Tasmanian Working with Vulnerable People registration (or the ability and willingness to obtain before commencement). A current Australian C class driver licence or manual vehicle licence. Salary details Appointment to this role will be at Academic Level A and will have a total remuneration package of up to $125,877 comprising base salary within the range of $83,198 to $107,587 plus 17% superannuation. How to Apply To apply online, please provide the following supporting documentation: Resume Cover letter outlining your suitability and motivation for the role Your responses to the success criteria from the Position Description below For further information about this position, please contact Doctor T M Rabiul Islam, Deputy Director, Ocean Seafaring, Rabiul.Islam@utas.edu.au Please visit https://www.utas.edu.au/jobs/applying for our guide to applying and details on the recruitment process. For current UTAS staff, in submitting your application, you acknowledge you have/intend to discuss your interest in this vacancy with your current manager and understand they may need to be contacted confidentially by the hiring manager as part of the assessment of your application. Please refer to the attached Position Description Below for full details. Position Description - Level-A - Associate Lecturer in Seafaring (504312).pdf Applications close Thursday 22 October 2026, 11.55pm As part of our commitment to a safe and inclusive workplace, employment history and police checks may be conducted as part of the selection process. We recognise that great candidates bring a range of experiences and may not meet every requirement in exactly the same way. If this opportunity interests you and you believe your skills and maritime experience could make a valuable contribution, we encourage you to apply. We welcome applications from international candidates, with visa sponsorship available for suitably qualified applicants. Advertised: 23 Sep 2026 Tasmania Standard Time Applications close: 22 Oct 2026 Tasmania Daylight Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App Send me jobs like these We will email you new jobs that match this search. Great, we can send you jobs like this, if this is your first time signing up, please check your inbox to confirm your subscription. The email address was invalid, please check for errors. You must agree to the privacy statement Subscribe Recaptcha Privacy agreement Search results Position Location Closes Associate Lecturer in Seafaring (Marine Engineering) Launceston 22 Oct 2026 Take your maritime career in a new direction, sharing your marine engineering knowledge and seafaring experience to educate and inspire the next generation of maritime professionals Expression of interest Loading... Current Opportunities Position Location Closes Associate Lecturer in Seafaring (Marine Engineering) Launceston 22 Oct 2026 Take your maritime career in a new direction, sharing your marine engineering knowledge and seafaring experience to educate and inspire the next generation of maritime professionals Powered by PageUp Authorised by the Director of Marketing. © 2025 University of Tasmania, Australia. ABN 30 764 374 782. CRICOS Provider Code 00586B Copyright statement Disclaimer Privacy Web accessibility"
