    "https://careers.utas.edu.au/en/job/503597/dean-and-head-of-school-law": {
      "title": "Current Vacancies - Jobs and Careers",
      "description": "Current Vacancies - Jobs and Careers | University of Tasmania Close Home Study with us Uni life Our research For community & partners About us Staff portal Student portal MyLO eStudent Search Close How can we help you? Search Back to Working at the University Jobs and Careers Looking to become part of a welcoming and collaborative community making a difference for Tasmania and the World? View our current career opportunities here. Home About us Working at the University Current job opportunities Applicant login Subscribe Search e.g. “Marketing”, “IMAS”, “Research” Filter results Work type Full time 1 Locations Hobart 1 Categories Professional Management 1 Dean and Head of School, Law Apply now Job no: 503597 Work type: Full time Location: Hobart Categories: Management The University of Tasmania acknowledges the Palawa/Pakana and Gadigal/Wangal people as the traditional custodians of the land, sea and waters of the areas upon which we live and work. We recognise their valuable contributions and deep connection to country and pay respect to Elders past and present. Shape the future of legal education and student success Strengthen research excellence and external partnerships Lead academic strategy, people, performance, and culture About the Opportunity The School of Law at the University of Tasmania is one of Australia’s oldest and most respected centres of legal education, with a proud history dating back to 1893. This is an exceptional opportunity for an accomplished academic leader to shape the future of a distinguished law school that is committed to excellence in legal education, impactful research, and meaningful engagement with the legal profession and wider community. As Dean and Head of School, you will provide strategic, academic, and operational leadership, fostering a culture of collaboration, innovation, and high performance. You will lead the School’s contribution to the University’s strategic goals, strengthen partnerships across government, industry and the legal sector, and support the development of graduates equipped to make a positive impact in Tasmania, Australia and beyond. This role offers the opportunity to build on a strong legacy while guiding the School into its next chapter of growth and influence. About the University The University of Tasmania is a mission-driven institution dedicated to making a difference for Lutruwita/Tasmania and a distinctive contribution from Tasmania to the world. As the sole university on the island, the University is deeply embedded in the social, economic, and environmental fabric of Tasmania, working in close partnership with communities, industry, and government to address key challenges in education, health, productivity, and climate action. The University takes pride in its place-based identity, leveraging Tasmania’s unique geography, culture, and resources to offer world-leading research and education. What We are Looking For We are seeking an outstanding academic leader with a strong track record of achievement in legal education, research, and engagement. You will bring the vision to shape the future of legal education, the leadership capability to foster a high-performing and collaborative culture, and the strategic insight to navigate a rapidly evolving higher education environment. With experience leading academic excellence, research growth, and operational performance, you will be skilled at building meaningful partnerships across the legal profession, government, industry, and the community. A deep understanding of the opportunities and challenges facing legal education, together with a commitment to innovation, student success, and institutional impact, will position you to lead the School's next chapter with confidence and ambition. What’s on Offer? As we look toward 2050, we remain committed to fostering excellence, collaboration, and transformative education - preparing graduates to navigate the challenges of a rapidly changing world, and shape its future. While remaining true to our Tasmanian roots, this career defining role will see you significantly contribute to our dedication to making a difference for, and from, Tasmania. In return for your experience, you’ll have access to an array of staff benefits and discounts including: 26 Weeks paid parental leave for primary care givers 17% Superannuation contribution Health and fitness membership options Accommodation and Car Hire discounts Health Insurance and Banking Novated Leasing How to Apply? If this role is of interest, we look forward to hearing from you. Please provide your up-to-date resume and: A 1-page cover letter outlining your motivation for the role at the University of Tasmania A separate response of up to 3-pages related to the role specific skills, knowledge and experience in the Position Description For further information about this position, please contact the Talent Acquisition team via email: TalentTeam@utas.edu.au Applications Close Friday the 30th of October at 11.55pm 2026 Position Description - Dean and Head of School Law - September 2026.pdf To be eligible for this position, you are required to have Australian citizenship, permanent residence or a current valid visa that allows you to fulfill the requirements of this role. As part of our commitment to a safe and inclusive workplace, employment history and police background checks may be conducted as part of the selection process. Advertised: 29 Sep 2026 Tasmania Standard Time Applications close: 30 Oct 2026 Tasmania Daylight Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App Send me jobs like these We will email you new jobs that match this search. Great, we can send you jobs like this, if this is your first time signing up, please check your inbox to confirm your subscription. The email address was invalid, please check for errors. You must agree to the privacy statement Subscribe Recaptcha Privacy agreement Search results Position Location Closes Dean and Head of School, Law Hobart 30 Oct 2026 Lead one of Australia’s oldest law schools, advancing excellence in legal education, research, and public impact. Expression of interest Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander Expression of Interest Are you an Aboriginal and/or Torres Strait Islander person looking for a dynamic employment opportunity that allows you to contribute and make a difference in your community? Current Opportunities Position Location Closes Dean and Head of School, Law Hobart 30 Oct 2026 Lead one of Australia’s oldest law schools, advancing excellence in legal education, research, and public impact. Powered by PageUp Authorised by the Director of Marketing. © 2025 University of Tasmania, Australia. ABN 30 764 374 782. CRICOS Provider Code 00586B Copyright statement Disclaimer Privacy Web accessibility"
    },
    "https://careers.utas.edu.au/en/job/504345/lecturer-in-nursing": {
      "title": "Lecturer in Nursing",
      "description": "Current Vacancies - Jobs and Careers | University of Tasmania Close Home Study with us Uni life Our research For community & partners About us Staff portal Student portal MyLO eStudent Search Close How can we help you? Search Back to Working at the University Jobs and Careers Looking to become part of a welcoming and collaborative community making a difference for Tasmania and the World? View our current career opportunities here. Home About us Working at the University Current job opportunities Applicant login Subscribe Search e.g. “Marketing”, “IMAS”, “Research” Filter results Work type Full time 1 Locations Burnie 1 Hobart 1 Launceston 1 Categories Academic Teaching Intensive 1 Lecturer in Nursing Apply now Job no: 504345 Work type: Full time Location: Hobart, Launceston, Burnie Categories: Teaching Intensive The University of Tasmania acknowledges the Palawa/Pakana and Gadigal/Wangal people as the traditional custodians of the land, sea and waters of the areas upon which we live and work. We recognise their valuable contributions and deep connection to country and pay respect to Elders past and present. Play a key role in preparing the next generation of nurses through high-quality teaching Contribute to innovation in learning and teaching to meet the health service delivery needs Full-time (or part time) ongoing position based in Hobart, Launceston or Burnie About the opportunity This is an exciting opportunity for an enthusiastic and motivated Registered Nurse to join a dynamic team in the  School of Nursing, which is part of the Health portfolio. The School of Nursing works in close partnership with Tasmanian and national healthcare partners to improve the health of Tasmanians and to educate a new generation of nurses with the capability in leadership and future-focused health care. We build our future workforce through innovative learning experiences using online and face-to-face delivery across our undergraduate and postgraduate courses. We foster a dynamic, highly engaged work environment. The School of Nursing sits within the Health portfolio, which has a focus on  evidence-based learning and teaching to educate a new generation of agile health professionals. With a strong background in nursing education, this position will contribute to student success through the educational preparation of pre-registration nursing students. You will support developing a positive culture that enables high levels of collaboration and productivity to support the vision of the University to deliver place-based education with high impact for Tasmania. What you’ll do: Make an effective and sustained contribution to the University in achieving its strategic objectives and fulfilling its operational responsibilities. Undertake high quality teaching in undergraduate and if applicable in postgraduate. Make an effective contribution in the teaching of undergraduate contemporary nursing curricula, including unit coordination, curriculum development, evaluation and using emerging and online technology. Contribute to collaborative research and/or scholarly contributions as relevant for qualifications and the University’s performance expectations for Level B. Contribute to productive and effective teams and partnerships inside the University and locally and nationally with the discipline, relevant interdisciplinary domains, profession, industry and/or wider community. Behave in a manner that aligns with the University of Tasmania Behaviour Policy and Statement of Values and promote a vibrant, positive campus culture with a focus on safety. ­ What we’re looking for: Current registration with Ahpra as a Registered Nurse with no conditions Post graduate nursing qualifications: Graduate certificate minimum. PhD or Masters level desirable. Experience in university-level learning and teaching, including experiences in undergraduate programs across all modes using contemporary teaching practices. Experience in managing and coordinating units and developing nursing curricula will be looked on favourably. Engagement with innovative approaches to learning and teaching, and the capacity to initiate and deliver quality improvement activities. Experience in nursing practice (with recency), including experience in a specialty area of nursing practice. Excellent interpersonal skills and a record of contributing effectively to building and maintaining productive and effective partnerships with the discipline, profession, industry (where relevant) and wider community. Evidence of evidence-based practice capability demonstrated by health service/program evaluation, publications, or direct experience in research projects will be looked on favourably. Salary details Appointment to this role will be at Academic Level B and will have a total remuneration package of up to $155,885, comprising base salary within the range of $114,766 to $133,235 plus 17% superannuation. How to Apply To apply online, please provide your CV with your registration number and a 1-2-page cover letter outlining your interest in the role, skills, capabilities and experience.  You do not need to separately address the Roles Specific Skills, Knowledge and Experience For further information about this position, please contact Grace Bennett-Daly, grace.bennettdaly@utas.edu.au Please visit https://www.utas.edu.au/jobs/applying for our guide to applying and details on the recruitment process. For current UTAS staff, in submitting your application, you acknowledge you have/intend to discuss your interest in this vacancy with your current manager and understand they may need to be contacted confidentially by the hiring manager as part of the assessment of your application. Please refer to the attached Position Description below for full details. Position Description - 504345 - Lecturer in Nursing.pdf Applications close Sunday, 18 October 2026, 11.55 pm The University of Tasmania acknowledges the Palawa/Pakana and Gadigal/Wangal people as the traditional custodians of the land, sea and waters of the areas upon which we live and work. We recognise their valuable contributions and deep connection to country and pay respect to Elders past and present. As part of our commitment to a safe and inclusive workplace, employment history and police checks may be conducted as part of the selection process. To be eligible for this position, you are required to hold Australian or New Zealand Citizenship, permanent residency or a valid visa that enables you to fulfil the requirements of this role. Advertised: 29 Sep 2026 Tasmania Standard Time Applications close: 18 Oct 2026 Tasmania Daylight Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App Send me jobs like these We will email you new jobs that match this search. Great, we can send you jobs like this, if this is your first time signing up, please check your inbox to confirm your subscription. The email address was invalid, please check for errors. You must agree to the privacy statement Subscribe Recaptcha Privacy agreement Search results Position Location Closes Lecturer in Nursing Hobart, Launceston, Burnie 18 Oct 2026 Seeking an enthusiastic and motivated lecturer to join the dynamic team at the School of Nursing Expression of interest Loading... Current Opportunities Position Location Closes Lecturer in Nursing Hobart, Launceston, Burnie 18 Oct 2026 Seeking an enthusiastic and motivated lecturer to join the dynamic team at the School of Nursing Powered by PageUp Authorised by the Director of Marketing. © 2025 University of Tasmania, Australia. ABN 30 764 374 782. CRICOS Provider Code 00586B Copyright statement Disclaimer Privacy Web accessibility"
    },
    "https://careers.utas.edu.au/en/job/504311/lecturersenior-lecturer-in-health-sociology-indigenous": {
      "title": "Lecturer/Senior Lecturer in Health Sociology (Indigenous)",
      "description": "Current Vacancies - Jobs and Careers | University of Tasmania Close Home Study with us Uni life Our research For community & partners About us Staff portal Student portal MyLO eStudent Search Close How can we help you? Search Back to Working at the University Jobs and Careers Looking to become part of a welcoming and collaborative community making a difference for Tasmania and the World? View our current career opportunities here. Home About us Working at the University Current job opportunities Applicant login Subscribe Search e.g. “Marketing”, “IMAS”, “Research” Filter results Work type Full time 1 Locations Hobart 1 Launceston 1 Statewide (all locations in Tasmania) 1 Categories Academic Teaching Intensive 1 Lecturer/Senior Lecturer in Health Sociology (Indigenous) Apply now Job no: 504311 Work type: Full time Location: Hobart, Launceston, Statewide (all locations in Tasmania) Categories: Teaching Intensive Teach Health Sociology across undergraduate and postgraduate programs Shape health research and education through Aboriginal knowledges and partnerships Full time, ongoing position based in Launceston or Hobart The University of Tasmania acknowledges the Palawa/Pakana and Gadigal/Wangal people as the traditional custodians of the land, sea and waters of the areas upon which we live and work. We pay our respects to Elders past and present who hold memories, knowledges and culture, and their enduring cultural practices of caring for Country that spans millennia. About the Opportunity As the Lecturer or Senior Lecturer in Health Sociology, you will deliver high-quality teaching across undergraduate and postgraduate programs, with a particular focus on embedding Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander knowledges and perspectives. Working collaboratively, you will contribute to teaching, research and partnerships that strengthen culturally responsive approaches to health and address health inequalities and inequities. In addition, you will: Embed Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander knowledges and perspectives across Sociology and Humanities and Social Sciences teaching. Contribute to teaching and curriculum development within nursing and allied health programs. Undertake high-quality research that contributes to the school’s research excellence and impact. Supervise research higher degree students and support their successful completion. Provide academic leadership and contribute to outstanding learning and teaching. Build productive relationships with Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander stakeholders, students, colleagues and community. Contribute to collaborative research, teaching and engagement across the University and with external partners. About the University At the University of Tasmania, we have a mission to make a difference for Tasmania, and from Tasmania. With our unique location and history, we have both an obligation and opportunity to make a global contribution. People are at the heart of that contribution. We focus on building a workplace culture where we come together in a way that empowers us all to connect, belong and grow. The University is one of the largest employers in our State, with campuses in three distinct regions: Hobart, Launceston, and Burnie. We also have a specialised health campus in Sydney. We strive to stimulate economic growth, lift literacy, improve health outcomes for Tasmania and nurture our environment as it nurtures us. We welcome enquiring minds, from near and far, to join us in our pursuit of the extraordinary. What’s Required For Level B (Lecturer) A PhD or equivalent qualification in a relevant social science field. A demonstrated record of, and a continuing commitment to research in the field of health sociology. University-level teaching experience across online and face-to-face delivery. Experience building and maintaining productive relationships with the discipline, health professions, Aboriginal and/or Torres Strait Islander people and/or wider community. Excellent written and verbal communication skills, with the ability to engage thoughtfully with students and staff from diverse backgrounds and cultures. The ability to work effectively both independently and collaboratively within discipline-based and multidisciplinary teams. For Level C (Senior Lecturer) (in addition to the above) A strong record in, and continuing commitment to, research that has made notable contributions to the field of health sociology. A record of research higher degree supervision and completions, relative to opportunity. Demonstrated achievement in University-level teaching and learning across different delivery modes. A record of contributing effectively to building and maintaining effective and productive links locally and nationally with the discipline, health professions, Aboriginal and/or Torres Strait Islander people, and/or wider community. This is an Identified Position. You must be an Aboriginal and/or Torres Strait Islander person to be considered for this opportunity. Candidates must provide eligibility documentation with their application. Please contact aboriginal.employment@utas.edu.au to discuss the documentation required. What’s on Offer? On offer is a full time, ongoing opportunity based in Launceston or Hobart with a competitive remuneration package. We value our staff and the important contributions they make to our community. You’ll have access to an array of staff benefits and discounts including: 26 weeks paid parental leave for primary care givers 5 days Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander Cultural leave annually 17% superannuation contribution Health and fitness membership options Accommodation and car hire discounts Health insurance and banking Novated Leasing Relocation support available for those considering a move to Tasmania Belong - Be You People are at the heart of everything we do. Our vision is to ensure that our people feel encouraged and supported to bring their whole selves to work. Fostering diversity and inclusion is our key priority, supporting our communities to come together in ways that empower connection, learning, belonging and growth. Celebrating the strengths that diversity brings will enable us to achieve our vision and mission. Applications are encouraged from all sectors of the community. Tell us how we can make this job work for you. Remuneration Appointment to this role will be at Academic Level B or C and will have a remuneration package of up to $184,076, comprising of a base salary within the range of $114,766 to $157,330 plus 17% superannuation. How to Apply? If this role is of interest, we look forward to hearing from you. Please provide your up-to-date resume A 1–2-page cover letter outlining your motivation for the role And a statement addressing the selection criteria by outlining how your skills and experience align with the role specific skills, knowledge and experience in the Position Description For further information about this position, please contact Professor Vaughan Higgins, Professor, Sociology at vaughan.higgins@utas.edu.au , Associate Professor Peta Cook, Associate Professor in Sociology at peta.cook@utas.edu.au or aboriginal.employment@utas.edu.au . Applications close 11:55pm Wednesday 28 th of October 2026 Lecturer-Senior Lecturer in Health Sociology (Indigenous) Position Description.pdf Looking for another Health Sociology opportunity? We are also recruiting for a Lecturer in Health Sociology. Take a look at our other role and explore which opportunity is the right fit for you. As part of our commitment to a safe and inclusive workplace, employment history and police checks may be conducted as part of the selection process. To be eligible for this position, you are required to hold Australian or New Zealand Citizenship, permanent residency or a valid visa that enables you to fulfil the requirements of this role. Advertised: 30 Sep 2026 Tasmania Standard Time Applications close: 28 Oct 2026 Tasmania Daylight Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App Send me jobs like these We will email you new jobs that match this search. Great, we can send you jobs like this, if this is your first time signing up, please check your inbox to confirm your subscription. The email address was invalid, please check for errors. You must agree to the privacy statement Subscribe Recaptcha Privacy agreement Search results Position Location Closes Lecturer/Senior Lecturer in Health Sociology (Indigenous) Hobart, Launceston, Statewide (all locations in Tasmania) 28 Oct 2026 Shape the future of Health Sociology through innovative teaching, impactful research and meaningful connections across health, education and community Expression of interest Loading... Current Opportunities Position Location Closes Lecturer/Senior Lecturer in Health Sociology (Indigenous) Hobart, Launceston, Statewide (all locations in Tasmania) 28 Oct 2026 Shape the future of Health Sociology through innovative teaching, impactful research and meaningful connections across health, education and community Powered by PageUp Authorised by the Director of Marketing. © 2025 University of Tasmania, Australia. ABN 30 764 374 782. CRICOS Provider Code 00586B Copyright statement Disclaimer Privacy Web accessibility"
    },
    "https://careers.utas.edu.au/en/job/504314/lecturer-in-health-sociology": {
      "title": "Lecturer in Health Sociology",
      "description": "Current Vacancies - Jobs and Careers | University of Tasmania Close Home Study with us Uni life Our research For community & partners About us Staff portal Student portal MyLO eStudent Search Close How can we help you? Search Back to Working at the University Jobs and Careers Looking to become part of a welcoming and collaborative community making a difference for Tasmania and the World? View our current career opportunities here. Home About us Working at the University Current job opportunities Applicant login Subscribe Search e.g. “Marketing”, “IMAS”, “Research” Filter results Work type Full time 1 Locations Launceston 1 Statewide (all locations in Tasmania) 1 Categories Academic Teaching Intensive 1 Lecturer in Health Sociology Apply now Job no: 504314 Work type: Full time Location: Launceston, Statewide (all locations in Tasmania) Categories: Teaching Intensive The University of Tasmania acknowledges the Palawa/Pakana and Gadigal/Wangal people as the traditional custodians of the land, sea and waters of the areas upon which we live and work. We recognise their valuable contributions and deep connection to country and pay respect to Elders past and present. Teach Health Sociology across undergraduate and postgraduate programs Embed cultural safety and Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander perspectives in health education Full time, ongoing position based in Launceston About the Opportunity As a Lecturer in Health Sociology you will work within a collaborative, multi-disciplinary team to deliver programs and teach students attending University of Tasmania campuses located in Launceston, Hobart, Burnie, and Sydney. This position is a teaching-intensive academic role with a particular focus on embedding cultural safety into the learning content within our contributions to the University’s nursing and allied health curriculum. The position will require success in teaching, research and service, with a research allocation designed to contribute to the School’s record of research excellence and impact in health sociology. About the University At the University of Tasmania, we have a mission to make a difference for Tasmania, and from Tasmania. With our unique location and history, we have both an obligation and opportunity to make a global contribution. People are at the heart of that contribution. We focus on building a workplace culture where we come together in a way that empowers us all to connect, belong and grow. The University is one of the largest employers in our State, with campuses in three distinct regions: Hobart, Launceston, and Burnie. We also have a specialised health campus in Sydney. We strive to stimulate economic growth, lift literacy, improve health outcomes for Tasmania and nurture our environment as it nurtures us. We welcome enquiring minds, from near and far, to join us in our pursuit of the extraordinary . What’s Required A PhD or equivalent in a relevant social science field. A demonstrated record of, and continuing commitment to, research in the field of health sociology. Experience in University-level teaching and learning across different delivery modes, including online and face-to-face. Teaching and/or research experience in cultural safety. Excellent written and verbal communication skills with the ability to relate effectively and thoughtfully to students and staff from a variety of backgrounds and cultures. A proven ability to work effectively independently and as part of discipline-based and multi-disciplinary teams. What’s on Offer? On offer is a full time, ongoing opportunity based in Launceston with a competitive remuneration package. We value our staff and the important contributions they make to our community. You’ll have access to an array of staff benefits and discounts including: 26 weeks paid parental leave for primary care givers 5 days Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander Cultural leave annually 17% superannuation contribution Health and fitness membership options Accommodation and car hire discounts Health insurance and banking Novated Leasing Relocation support available for those considering a move to Tasmania Belong – Be You People are at the heart of everything we do. Our vision is to ensure that our people feel encouraged and supported to bring their whole selves to work. Fostering diversity and inclusion is our key priority, supporting our communities to come together in ways that empower connection, learning, belonging and growth. Celebrating the strengths that diversity brings will enable us to achieve our vision and mission. Applications are encouraged from all sectors of the community. Tell us how we can make this job work for you. Remuneration Appointment to this role will be at Academic Level B and will have a remuneration package of up to $155,885 comprising of a base salary within the range of $114,766 to $133,235 plus 17% superannuation. How to Apply? If this role is of interest, we look forward to hearing from you. Please provide your up-to-date resume A 1–2-page cover letter outlining your motivation for the role A statement addressing the selection criteria by outlining how your skills and experience align with the role specific skills, knowledge and experience in the Position Description For further information about this position, please contact Professor Vaughan Higgins, Professor, Sociology at vaughan.higgins@utas.edu.au Associate Professor Peta Cook, Associate Professor in Sociology at peta.cook@utas.edu.au Applications close 11:55pm Wednesday 28th of October 2026 Lecturer in Health Sociology - Position Description (504314).pdf We are also recruiting for a Lecturer/Senior Lecturer in Health Sociology (Indigenous).  Take a look at our other role and explore which opportunity is the right fit for you. As part of our commitment to a safe and inclusive workplace, employment history and police checks may be conducted as part of the selection process. To be eligible for this position, you are required to hold Australian or New Zealand Citizenship, permanent residency or a valid visa that enables you to fulfil the requirements of this role. Advertised: 30 Sep 2026 Tasmania Standard Time Applications close: 28 Oct 2026 Tasmania Daylight Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App Send me jobs like these We will email you new jobs that match this search. Great, we can send you jobs like this, if this is your first time signing up, please check your inbox to confirm your subscription. The email address was invalid, please check for errors. You must agree to the privacy statement Subscribe Recaptcha Privacy agreement Search results Position Location Closes Lecturer in Health Sociology Launceston, Statewide (all locations in Tasmania) 28 Oct 2026 Shape the future of Health Sociology through transformative teaching, culturally responsive education and impactful research that addresses health inequalities Expression of interest Loading... Current Opportunities Position Location Closes Lecturer in Health Sociology Launceston, Statewide (all locations in Tasmania) 28 Oct 2026 Shape the future of Health Sociology through transformative teaching, culturally responsive education and impactful research that addresses health inequalities Powered by PageUp Authorised by the Director of Marketing. © 2025 University of Tasmania, Australia. ABN 30 764 374 782. CRICOS Provider Code 00586B Copyright statement Disclaimer Privacy Web accessibility"
    },
    "https://careers.utas.edu.au/en/job/504319/senior-administrative-officer": {
      "title": "Current Vacancies - Jobs and Careers",
      "description": "Current Vacancies - Jobs and Careers | University of Tasmania Close Home Study with us Uni life Our research For community & partners About us Staff portal Student portal MyLO eStudent Search Close How can we help you? Search Back to Working at the University Jobs and Careers Looking to become part of a welcoming and collaborative community making a difference for Tasmania and the World? View our current career opportunities here. Home About us Working at the University Current job opportunities Applicant login Subscribe Search e.g. “Marketing”, “IMAS”, “Research” Filter results Work type Full time 1 Locations Hobart 1 Launceston 1 Categories Professional Administration 1 Senior Administrative Officer Apply now Job no: 504319 Work type: Full time Location: Hobart, Launceston Categories: Administration The University of Tasmania acknowledges the Palawa/Pakana and Gadigal/Wangal people as the traditional custodians of the land, sea and waters of the areas upon which we live and work. We recognise their valuable contributions and deep connection to country and pay respect to Elders past and present. Provide high-level analysis and advice to support academic workload allocation, staffing and operational planning Build strong relationships across the school while contributing to effective processes, projects and decision-making Full time, ongoing role based in either Launceston or Hobart About the opportunity The Senior Administration Officer provides high-level administrative and analytical support across a range of School activities, contributing to operational planning, priority projects and the effective delivery of key administrative functions. A key focus of the role is the effective administration of academic workload allocation. Working under the broad direction of the Business Manager and closely with senior academic staff, you will analyse workload requirements, provide informed advice and generate and maintain accurate workload allocation records. You will draw on your understanding of staff expertise, relevant systems, policies and guidelines, and the school’s budget context to support effective planning and decision-making. You will bring strong analytical skills, sound judgement and a collaborative approach, building effective relationships across the school to deliver high-quality operational outcomes. Your contribution will support both effective academic workload planning and the responsible management of the school’s budget. What you’ll do: Provide high-level administrative support and advice to the School Workload Allocation Committee. Analyse academic workloads using relevant agreements, workload models, timetables and student load data to identify teaching gaps and workload imbalances and provide advice on staffing requirements across undergraduate and postgraduate programs. Support effective School operations across recruitment, work health and safety, space management, risk and procurement. Coordinate and collaborate with administrative staff to deliver effective operational support, including casual workload allocation, timesheet approvals, procurement, space management and contractor liaison. Analyse and report on key performance indicators, workload allocation and expenditure against budget. Work collaboratively with Health Operations and Support and School professional staff to improve systems, policies and procedures, contribute to projects and deliver School operational requirements. What we’re looking for: Completion of a degree/diploma in a relevant field with subsequent relevant work experience or an equivalent combination of relevant experience and/or education/training. Well-developed analytical, verbal and written communication skills and the capacity to interpret and apply policy and institutional knowledge, prepare reports, keep accurate records and maintain confidentiality. Highly developed administration and organisational skills, with a demonstrated ability to work flexibly and to exercise initiative and judgement on work methods and task sequence in order to meet deadlines in a high-volume work environment. Proven capacity and confidence to build and maintain relationships, work effectively as part of a team and effectively communicate and negotiate with a diverse range of stakeholders at all levels Demonstrated, high level computer skills including excellent spreadsheet, information management, email and word processing skills, ability to retrieve and analyse information from corporate data systems and experience in the use of Office 365 applications, including Teams and OneDrive. Qualifications and Licences Current ‘C’ class driver’s licence Salary details Appointment to this role will be at HEO 6 and will have a total remuneration package of up to $116,872 comprising base salary within the range of $92,120 to $99,891 plus 17% superannuation. How to Apply To apply online, please provide your CV and 1-2-page cover letter outlining your interest in the role, skills, capabilities and experience.  You do not need to separately address the success profile. For further information about this position, please contact Anthony McHugh, Business Manager - Nursing, Anthony.McHugh@utas.edu.au (0419334129) Please visit https://www.utas.edu.au/jobs/applying for our guide to applying and details on the recruitment process. Please refer to the attached Position Description Below for full details. Position Description (504319) Senior Administrative Officer.pdf Applications close Sunday, 11 October 2026, 11.55pm As part of our commitment to a safe and inclusive workplace, employment history and police checks may be conducted as part of the selection process. To be eligible for this position, you are required to hold Australian or New Zealand Citizenship, permanent residency or a valid visa that enables you to fulfil the requirements of this role. Advertised: 30 Sep 2026 Tasmania Standard Time Applications close: 12 Oct 2026 Tasmania Daylight Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App Send me jobs like these We will email you new jobs that match this search. Great, we can send you jobs like this, if this is your first time signing up, please check your inbox to confirm your subscription. The email address was invalid, please check for errors. You must agree to the privacy statement Subscribe Recaptcha Privacy agreement Search results Position Location Closes Senior Administrative Officer Hobart, Launceston 12 Oct 2026 Play a key role in supporting the School of Nursing through effective academic workload planning, analysis and operational decision-making Expression of interest Loading... Current Opportunities Position Location Closes Senior Administrative Officer Hobart, Launceston 12 Oct 2026 Play a key role in supporting the School of Nursing through effective academic workload planning, analysis and operational decision-making Powered by PageUp Authorised by the Director of Marketing. © 2025 University of Tasmania, Australia. ABN 30 764 374 782. CRICOS Provider Code 00586B Copyright statement Disclaimer Privacy Web accessibility"
