    "https://careers.utas.edu.au/jobs/residential-communities-officer-launceston-tas-australia": {
      "title": "Residential Communities Officer",
      "description": "Residential Communities Officer - Launceston, TAS, Australia Skip to main content Visit UTAS My Profile Kiosk mode Jobs and Careers Looking to become part of a welcoming and collaborative community making a difference for Tasmania and the World? View our current career opportunities here. Residential Communities Officer 504238 Launceston, TAS Full time Closing on: 4 Oct 2026 Add to favourites View favourites Strong interpersonal and communication skills, with the ability to build trust Reliability and flexibility to work shifts outside standard hours Ongoing, Full Time based in Launceston The University of Tasmania acknowledges the Palawa/Pakana and Gadigal/Wangal people as the traditional custodians of the land, sea and waters of the areas upon which we live and work. We recognise their valuable contributions and deep connection to country and pay respect to Elders past and present. About the opportunity The Residential Communities Officer (RCO) is a key student-facing role within the University's Campus Accommodation portfolio, supporting students living across its residential communities in Hobart, Sandy Bay, Launceston, and Burnie. The role provides high-quality support to a diverse residential population, including both domestic and international students. The RCO plays an important role in enhancing the student residential experience by supporting students' transition into residential life, fostering a welcoming and inclusive community, and promoting student wellbeing, engagement, and academic success. Working closely with students, the RCO responds to a broad range of needs and concerns with empathy, professionalism, and a student-centred approach. Guided by the Dean of Students and aligned with the Residential Communities Strategy 2025-2030, the RCO contributes to the development of safe, vibrant, and distinctly Tasmanian residential communities where students can thrive. The role supports a collegiate living model that promotes belonging, personal growth, shared experiences, and student leadership, underpinned by principles of resident focus, collaboration, innovation, and sustainability. The position requires flexibility, including participation in a roster that includes fixed shifts extending to 7.00 pm and occasional Saturday work on a rotational basis. What you’ll do: Ensure students living in residential colleges feel safe and well supported through positive, empathetic and timely responses from RCOs to any concerns raised. Regular one-on-one pastoral care meetings conducted with student residents, with comprehensive case notes maintained and appropriate referrals made to internal and external support services where required. Demonstrated sound judgment through effective risk assessment and appropriate responses to student behaviours and incidents, including critical incidents, in accordance with established procedures and with a strong focus on student welfare. Support the Dean of Students through regular engagement with student representative bodies and student-led groups, contributing to the development and delivery of student engagement programs, events, and initiatives that foster a thriving, inclusive community and a strong sense of belonging among all residents. What we’re looking for: Demonstrated excellent cultural competence, with proven experience effectively supporting and engaging people from diverse cultural backgrounds and communities. Demonstrated ability to respond to complex and critical incidents using a people-centred, safety-first approach, in accordance with Work Health and Safety legislation and University policies and procedures. Demonstrated excellent interpersonal and communication skills, with a strong people-centred approach, including the ability to engage and present to large groups, act as a positive role model for student residents, and effectively negotiate and resolve conflicts A degree qualification and relevant experience in a human services or education setting, or an equivalent combination of relevant experience, education, and training. Current Tasmanian Registration to Work with Vulnerable People (Employment status) and a valid driver's licence. First Aid and Mental Health First Aid qualifications are desirable Salary details Appointment to this role will be at HEO 6 and will have a total remuneration package of up to $116,872 comprising base salary within the range of $92,120 to $99,891 plus 17% superannuation. How to Apply To apply online, please provide your CV and 1-2-page cover letter outlining your interest in the role, skills, capabilities and experience.  You do not need to separately address the success profile. For further information about this position, please contact Suzi Singline, Dean of Students - Investigator Hall at Suzanne.Singline@utas.edu.au or 03 63243305. Please visit https://www.utas.edu.au/jobs/applying for our guide to applying and details on the recruitment process. For current UTAS staff, in submitting your application, you acknowledge you have/intend to discuss your interest in this vacancy with your current manager and understand they may need to be contacted confidentially by the hiring manager as part of the assessment of your application. Please refer to the attached Position Description Below for full details. Position Description - 504238 - Residential Communities Officer.pdf Applications close Sunday, 4 October 2026, 11.55pm As part of our commitment to a safe and inclusive workplace, employment history and police checks may be conducted as part of the selection process. To be eligible for this position, you are required to hold Australian or New Zealand Citizenship, permanent residency or a valid visa that enables you to fulfil the requirements of this role. Back to job search Recommended Jobs Executive Director The Underwood Institute for Education Hobart, TAS. Statewide (all locations in Tasmania), TAS Management Lead the Underwood Institute for Education to drive educational impact, partnership and system transformation across Tasmania. Learning & Research Librarian Hobart, TAS. Launceston, TAS Information Technology, Library Make a meaningful impact by empowering students, staff and researchers with inclusive learning and research support that enhances their information literacy and academic success. Senior Lecturer in Nursing Hobart, TAS. Launceston, TAS Teaching Intensive Share your nursing expertise, inspire the next generation and help shape the future of Tasmania’s nursing workforce © 2026  University of Tasmania, Australia. ABN 30 764 374 782. CRICOS Provider Code 00586B Cookie Preferences Copyright Statement Disclaimer Privacy Web accessibility Candidate Details We use cookies Some are necessary to operate the website and its functions. Others help personalise, improve content and services to show you the most relevant job opportunities. With the decision \"Accept essential only\" we will respect your privacy and will not set cookies that aren't necessary for the operation of the site. Accept essential only Accept all"
